export const RULES = `- Rules: prefer a rule book for behaviour. A rule book is an "msm:rules" element, behaviour without code that reads as sentences in MSM's Rule book: { "format": 1, "counters": [{ "name", "scope": "player" | "mob" | "world", "start" }], "rules": [{ "key" (a to z, 0 to 9 and _, at most 32 characters, unique in the book), "name", "enabled", "when": { "trigger", "subject", "options" }, "only_if": { "match": "all" | "any", "conditions": [{ "condition", "not", ... }] }, "do": [{ "step", ... }] }] }. When the user asks for something to happen (a block used, broken or stepped on, an item used, a mob hurt or dying, a player joining, every few seconds), write a rule book rather than a script, and rather than hand-made events whenever it needs logic (a condition, a chance, a counter, a repeat or a wait). Write a script only for what the catalogue below cannot do. Put new rules in the project's book, "<namespace>:rules", creating it when there is none. Never add the trigger component or the event a rule needs to its subject yourself, and never write the event "<subject id>_rules_<trigger>" or a "<namespace>:rules/..." script by hand: MSM compiles the books when it checks and exports, and a hand-made event on the same trigger runs first. Catalogue ids (use no others): triggers ${list(RULES_PROMPT_IDS.triggers)}; steps ${list(RULES_PROMPT_IDS.steps)}; conditions ${list(RULES_PROMPT_IDS.conditions)}; values ${list(RULES_PROMPT_IDS.values)} (a number field takes a number or { "value": <id>, ... }, "math" at most two deep; a text field takes a list of strings and values); places ${list(RULES_PROMPT_IDS.places)} (a name, or { "place": "player_looking" | "offset", ... }); who ${list(RULES_PROMPT_IDS.who)}. Block, container, machine and portal triggers need a project block as subject (containers and machines with their component), item and thrown triggers a project item, mob triggers a project mob, effect triggers a project effect, key triggers a project key bind and vehicle triggers a project vehicle ("vehicle_fired" needs a weapon), "command_used" a project command ("msm:command"; one that runs a function gets its rules as a script); "any_" triggers take a vanilla or project id, or no subject for every one; player and world triggers have none. A trigger has only its own actors (a random tick has no player), trigger options are in seconds ("interval_seconds", "cooldown_seconds") and are shared by every rule on one subject and trigger, and stop_normal_action works only on ${list(RULES_PROMPT_CANCELLABLE)}; stop_placing works only on "any_block_placed", stop_explosion, keep_blocks_safe and the condition caused_by (a mob id or "block") only on "explosion"; the value "facing" is words (north to down) for text fields and is_facing, never a number. Plain or script: a rule on a project subject with no "only_if" whose every step is ${list(RULES_PROMPT_PLAIN_STEPS)}, with plain numbers and an actor the trigger has, is plain and becomes events on the subject; any other rule (and every rule sharing its subject and trigger) compiles to a generated script, which the user can read and take over with "Copy as my own script". Limits: steps nest at most 3 levels deep, a rule has at most 50 steps, repeat runs at most 100 times, a radius is at most 64 blocks, explosion power 0 to 10 and drop_item at most 64. Tell the user plainly: a rule that gives a thing a new trigger needs a game restart, while changes to rules on a trigger it already has reload; rules need the Fabric mod jar, and vanilla packs carry none ("rules.needs-jar"); rules that need a script are left out of the Java project export ("rules.not-in-eject"), while plain rules are in it as events. Key binds and HUD elements need the jar too ("controls.needs-jar"), as do vehicles ("vehicles.needs-jar"); all three are left out of the Java project export ("export.not-in-eject"). Prefer the Rules templates (${list(RULES_PROMPT_TEMPLATES)}) and read template_data or kind_schema for "msm:rules" before writing a book; run_checks compiles the books, and their problems are "rules." codes that point at the rule and step.`;
